WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.570
Industrial network traffic is a foundational telemetry source.

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:06.600
It shows which devices communicate, the protocols they use,

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.400
the direction and timing of connections,

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.060
and in some cases the industrial operations represented by those messages.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:15.695
Traffic at trust boundaries is especially useful for understanding movement between

00:00:15.695 --> 00:00:16.300
enterprise,

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:17.700
site, and control functions.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:22.035
Controllers and PLC-related communications can reveal logic transfers, mode changes,

00:00:22.035 --> 00:00:23.200
programming sessions,

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:27.055
and unusual peers when the protocol and collection point provide that visibility.

00:00:27.055 --> 00:00:31.560
HMIs and SCADA applications may record operator logons, alarms, commands,

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.155
application faults, and communication loss.

00:00:34.155 --> 00:00:38.135
Engineering workstations offer high-value evidence because they contain privileged tools.

00:00:38.135 --> 00:00:43.140
Endpoint, application, and authentication logs can show users, processes, removable media,

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.990
project changes, and connections to control devices.

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:49.040
Collection must be tested to avoid affecting supported applications.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.868
Historians provide process trends and operating context.

00:00:51.868 --> 00:00:55.850
They can help investigators compare a cyber observation with changes in equipment state or

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:56.570
production.

00:00:56.570 --> 00:01:01.405
Historian access logs and replication paths may also reveal cross-boundary activity.

00:01:01.405 --> 00:01:06.015
Remote-access gateways, VPNs, jump hosts, and vendor platforms should record identity,

00:01:06.015 --> 00:01:09.595
session times, destination, approval, and sensitive actions where feasible.

00:01:09.595 --> 00:01:13.205
Firewalls and network devices provide permitted and denied connections,

00:01:13.205 --> 00:01:16.220
configuration changes, link status, and boundary events.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:20.560
Identity systems contribute authentication, privilege, and account-lifecycle evidence.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.000
Telemetry quality matters more than raw volume.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:27.555
Time synchronization, asset identity, retention, integrity, access control,

00:01:27.555 --> 00:01:30.555
and clear ownership determine whether records support investigation.

00:01:30.555 --> 00:01:34.435
Build a source map that explains what each system can report, what it cannot see,

00:01:34.435 --> 00:01:37.700
how long data is retained, and who responds when the source fails.
